nal Stadium, Kasumigaoka, Shinjuku, Tokyo"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/19000711"} "46 from 29 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} [:em {} "Track &amp; Field News"] " noted in their Olympic preview that it was unusual to select a teenager as the favorite, but such was the strength of 19-year-old American " [:a {:href "/athletes/2506190"} "Athing Mu"] " that they picked her as the top choice for the 800 metres. Behind her other favorites were the Brits " [:a {:href "/athletes/142960"} "Jemma Reekie"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/142966"} "Keely Hodgkinson"] " and Cuba’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/123270"} "Rose Mary Almanza"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The only surprise of the heats and semis was that Almanza failed to get out of the third semi-final when she finished fourth. Britain qualified three runners for the final with " [:a {:href "/athletes/142952"} "Alexandra Bell"] " joining Reekie and Hodgkinson, while Mu was joined by her American teammate " [:a {:href "/athletes/147127"} "Raevyn Rogers"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final saw Mu take the lead as the runners broke for the rail off the first turn, followed by Jamaican " [:a {:href "/athletes/130727"} "Natoya Goule"] ". Mu continued to lead to the bell, running a solid pace, but not blazingly fast, at 57.82 for the first lap. Mu increased the pace on the penultimate turn, opening small gaps among the finalists."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Mu was never passed. On the final turn she stepped it up and opened her margin, having a comfortable lead on the straight as she won gold in 1:55.21, negative splitting her race at 57.82 / 57.39. Behind her, Hodgkinson won silver with a British record of 1:55.88 while Rogers put two Americans on the podium with her bronze medal in a time of 1:56.81."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Mu’s gold medal at age 19-057 made her the second youngest woman to win the Olympic 800, after Kenya’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/114401"} "Pamela Jelimo"] " in 2008 at age 18-256. Mu would add a second gold medal a few days later when she anchored the USA women to gold in the 4 x 400 relay."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3875"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/2506190"} "Athing Mu"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "2:01.10 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "1:58.07 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "1:55.21 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2005"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/142966"} "Keely Hodgkinson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "2:01.59 (2 h4)"] [:td {} "1:59.12 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "1:55.88 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3887"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/147127"} "Raevyn Rogers"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "2:01.42 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "1:59.28 (3 h3)"] [:td {} "1:56.81 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2024"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/142960"} "Jemma Reekie"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "1:59.97 (1 h6)"] [:td {} "1:59.77 (2 h1)"] [:td